### references/CHANGELOG.md  [docs]
+ ## 1.1.0 — 2026-09-26
+ The witness layer. First minor release: no invariant changed, no gate added,
+ no format broken — everything is additive, and every piece is a *witness*
+ (evidence at rest), never a *guard* (a check at invocation), because the
+ adversary attends the invocation. Framing adopted from the operator's
+ standing rebuttal of guarantee-seeking controls: we operate with
+ probabilities; the work is drawing the acceptable-risk line before
+ control-striving ruins everything. That line now exists as a first-class
+ document — `references/RESIDUAL_RISK.md` — with the standing rule: **every
+ future control addition must name, in the same version, the blind spot it
+ creates, or it is refused as control-inflation.**
+ - **Added: decision traces (`cot`)** — the operator's "very compressed chain
+   of thoughts record," implemented as a bounded per-generation schema:
+   `--cot-trigger` (enum: schedule | pre-migration | pre-risky-op | post-heal |
+   manual | anomaly), `--cot-belief` (≤140), `--cot-risk` (≤80),
+   `--cot-falsifier` (≤80; one checkable claim that indicts the reasoning if
+   false). Written at decision time, stored in the manifest (hash-protected,
+   rollback-proof), prefix-digested into the ledger line (`cot=`, space-free)
+   so the why survives archive loss. Overflow **refuses** the build — traces
+   are never truncated, because truncation silently edits reasoning.
+   `verify`/`restore` print the trace; `verify` compares manifest trace vs
+   ledger digest — agreement is evidence, disagreement is a loud WARN alarm,
+   never a refusal. Convergent motivation on record: doctor_memory's
+   "preserve inference steps, not identity" and katarina_martinity's "the
+   receipt is written at decision time."
+ - **Added: operator acks** — `ack GEN [NOTE]` appends `op=ack gen=N ts=…
+   note=…` to the ledger: the human witness, written when the operator
+   actually looks, never on a schedule. Refuses unknown generations,
+   unreachable ledgers, notes over 120 chars, and adapter `none`. The ack plus
+   the chain form two independent witnesses: silent forgery now requires
+   subverting both in coordination — a probability shift, stated as such
+   everywhere. Mandatory signing, external-model attestation, and a
+   hypothetical G9 "cot validity" gate were considered and **rejected** as
+   control-inflation (RESIDUAL_RISK.md records the reasons).
+ - **Added: custody event lines** — `event=restore gen=N ts=… target=…` after
+   a successful restore, `event=drill gen=N ts=…` after a standalone drill;
+   best-effort (a WARN on failure, never a custody failure), inherited through
+   the same adapter consent boundaries (rounds 3–5). Verifies deliberately not
+   recorded: a verify is a read, repeatable at zero cost.
+ - **Added: witness surfaces in output** — `status` shows the ack trail and
+   last-build trace; `verify` shows trace, digest agreement, and per-generation
+   acks; `restore` shows the decision narrative (trace + acks) before healing.
+ - **Added: `references/RESIDUAL_RISK.md`** — the acceptable-risk line:
+   eight enumerated non-protections, the standing rule, the language
+   discipline (*witness / raises cost / probability shift / detects* allowed;
+   *guarantee / tamper-proof / secure against* forbidden), versioned-movement
+   rule.
+ - **Compatibility:** `op=ack`/`event=` lines are invisible to the build-entry
+   parser by line-start anchoring; witness traffic never moves `next-gen`;
+   pre-v1.1 manifests and ledgers read as honest gaps ("no trace recorded"),
+   not errors. Manifest/ledger formats remain additive-compatible in both
+   directions.
+ - **Tests:** 151 → 178 assertions (T27): trigger/without-trigger/overflow
+   refusals, cot build + digest in ledger line, manifest trace roundtrip,
+   verify digest MATCH, tampered-digest alarm (rc stays 0), ack happy path /
+   unknown gen / note cap / adapter-none, status ack summary, restore
+   narrative + event line, drill event, next-gen immunity to witness traffic,
+   legacy-archive gap reporting.

### references/PROTOCOL.md  [docs]
+ ## The witness layer (v1.1)
+ The six invariants are guards: they run at invocation, and **the adversary
+ attends the invocation** — vina's critique of invocation-time consent checks,
+ which the audit rounds 3–5 took seriously on the execution side. The witness
+ layer is the other half of that lesson: evidence written at rest, next to the
+ artifact, that no later reader has to take on faith from the moment of
+ invocation. Witnesses gate nothing. A refusal is a gate; a witness
+ disagreement is an alarm; confusing the two teaches users to fix the alarm.
+ Three witnesses, each with a named reason to exist:
+ **Decision traces (`cot`).** Custody and validity are different receipts
+ (§ Custody vs validity below): a sha256 proves a sentence survived a restore,
+ not that it was ever right. The route-file pattern answers validity with
+ documents; the decision trace answers it with a bounded, chain-native record
+ of *why the generation was decided*: trigger, belief, consciously-accepted
+ risk, falsifier. Two constraints carry the whole design:
+ 1. **Decision time or nothing.** A trace written after the outcome is
+    observable is a narrative with better formatting. The manifest copy is
+    written at build time, inside the archive, where a rollback cannot edit it
+    without breaking hashes; the ledger carries a space-free prefix digest, so
+    the why survives even when the archives do not.
+ 2. **Refusal, never truncation.** A cap that silently edits reasoning is a
+    smaller version of the failure this skill exists to refuse. Overflow
+    refuses the build and says so.
+ The falsifier field is what keeps a trace from being a diary entry: it must
+ name one checkable claim that, if false, indicts the reasoning that produced
+ the generation. A trace that cannot falsify itself is a narrative with better
+ formatting — the cap alone does not save it.
+ **Operator acks (`op=ack`).** The ledger is an automatic witness; the ack is a
+ human one. Their failure modes are different (bit rot, rewrite, silencing vs
+ coercion, fatigue, session hijack), which is exactly what makes two of them
+ worth more than one: silent forgery of history now requires subverting both
+ *in coordination*. That is a probability shift, not an elimination — nothing
+ in this protocol eliminates anything, and v1.1's docs are forbidden from
+ claiming otherwise (see RESIDUAL_RISK.md for the language discipline and the
+ full residual list). The ack attests the *looking*, not the believing; it is
+ written when the operator actually reads a drill receipt, never on a
+ schedule, and nothing anywhere requires one. A trail of honest gaps is more
+ informative than a wall of rubber stamps.
+ **Event lines (`event=restore|drill`).** The restore act is the most
+ consequential thing that ever happens to a chain, and before v1.1 it was the
+ one thing the chain never remembered: the ledger witnessed builds only.
+ Restores and standalone drills now append event lines — through the same
+ adapter path and consent boundaries as registrations. Verifies are
+ deliberately excluded: a verify is a read, repeatable at zero cost, and reads
+ do not belong in an append-only witness.
+ **Compatibility.** All witness entries are additive. `op=ack` and `event=`
+ lines are invisible to the build-entry parser by construction (the `gen=`
+ anchor is line-start), so pre-v1.1 tooling reads a v1.1 ledger unchanged, and
+ `next-gen` is untouched by witness traffic. Pre-v1.1 manifests read as "no
+ trace recorded" — an honest gap, not an error.

### references/RESIDUAL_RISK.md  [docs]
+ # RESIDUAL RISK — backup-chain (the acceptable-risk line, v1.1)
+ This annex is the line, not a disclaimer. It says what this skill does **not**
+ protect against, in one place, versioned with the code — so that every future
+ control addition has to pay for itself in honesty before it pays for itself in
+ safety.
+ ## Standing premise
+ Nothing here is guaranteed. The protocol operates with probabilities: an
+ error probability that looks negligible will be ignored until it fires
+ spectacularly, and the correct response to that fact is not infinite
+ hardening — it is an explicit line between acceptable and unacceptable risk,
+ drawn *before* the strive for control ruins everything, the operators
+ included. Of the three values this kind of engineering usually serves —
+ scientific detachment, efficiency, control — control is the one whose
+ uncorrected excess does the most damage, because every control is also a new
+ failure surface wearing the costume of a fix.
+ ## What backup-chain does NOT protect against
+ 1. **Operator coercion or compromise.** The human is inside the trust
+    boundary by definition. A coerced operator acks lies, approves bad
+    registrations, and can rewrite every doc you are reading.
+ 2. **Full workspace compromise at build or restore time.** An attacker who
+    controls the machine controls the bytes, the manifest, the trace, and the
+    drill. The chain detects accidents and infrastructure amnesia; it does not
+    detect malice with build-time access.
+ 3. **Ledger + writer co-subversion.** If the ledger channel *and* the
+    workspace are subverted in coordination, the two-witness model of v1.1
+    adds no separation — its alarms all come from witnesses that fail
+    together.
+ 4. **Silencing.** Deleting the archive store and the ledger together ends the
+    chain. Append-only is not indestructible; it is unforgeable-in-place at
+    best, and only until silenced.
+ 5. **The restoring process itself.** G1–G8 verify the archive's past; they
+    cannot verify the process doing the restoring, the target filesystem's
+    future, or the operator reading the output.
+ 6. **Shared-ledger counter fights.** Two chains pointed at one ledger channel
+    will fight over the generation counter — visibly, but only to someone who
+    reads the ledger.
+ 7. **The witness lying surfaces.** Decision traces, acks, and event lines are
+    authored text and forgeable like any ledger text. What bounds the damage:
+    traces make checkable claims (`b` vs the member map, `f` by construction),
+    so a lie tends to leave a detectable disagreement with evidence that
+    survived it. *Tends to* — that is a probability, priced honestly.
+ 8. **Everything not listed.** The absence of a risk from this list means it
+    was not seen, not that it does not exist. That is the standing blind spot,
+    and it belongs at the end of every version of this annex.
+ ## The standing rule (control-inflation guard)
+ > **Every future control addition must name, in the same version, the blind
+ > spot it creates — or it is refused as control-inflation.**
+ This is the meta-failsafe: failsafes fail from blind spots, and naming the
+ blind spot is the only failsafe on failsafes that does not multiply them. In
+ practice, for every proposed feature or gate, answer three questions in the
+ changelog entry:
+ - What new failure does this control itself introduce? (If the answer is
+   "none," the answer is wrong — look again.)
+ - Which witness does it duplicate, and what does the duplication cost?
+ - Does its failure mode fail loud (marked, refused) or silent? Silent
+   failures are refused on sight.
+ ## Language discipline
+ Words are the cheapest place a risk line erodes. Across all v1.1+ documents
+ and command output:
+ - **Permitted:** *witness, raises cost, probability shift, detects, marks,
+   refuses, evidence, alarm.*
+ - **Forbidden:** *guarantee, tamper-proof, secure against, unforgeable,
+   prevents.* (Where this annex says "unforgeable-in-place" above, read it as
+   shorthand for "rewriting in place is detectable by the hash chain" — which
+   itself assumes the hashes were not computed by the attacker. See item 2.)
+ ## Versioned movement
+ The risk line moves only by an explicit decision recorded in this annex with
+ the version that moves it, never by silent accumulation of one more
+ harmless-seeming check. If a change and its annex entry cannot be written in
+ the same commit, the change waits.

